 "findings": {
  "lab": [
   "Los Angeles (METR-LA): at 60 minutes the best is D2STGNN at 3.779 mph mean absolute error, against 6.506 for persistence; the best zero-shot model is Chronos-2, all roads together (zero-shot) at 6.343.",
   "San Francisco Bay Area (PEMS-BAY): at 60 minutes the best is STID at 1.234 mph mean absolute error, against 2.05 for persistence; the best zero-shot model is Chronos-2, all roads together (zero-shot) at 1.959.",
   "Dhaka (DhaTraX, three camera sites, one day each), the best model other than persistence: at 15 minutes Chronos-Bolt-small (zero-shot) 2.225 km/h against persistence 2.385 (better); at 30 minutes Chronos-2 (zero-shot) 2.41 km/h against persistence 2.623 (better); at 60 minutes Chronos-2 (zero-shot) 2.877 km/h against persistence 2.717 (not better)."
  ],
  "unseen_city": "XGBoost trained on METR-LA (Los Angeles) and run unchanged on PEMS-BAY (San Francisco Bay Area) scores 2.76 mph at 60 minutes, against 2.35 when trained on PEMS-BAY itself: moving cities costs 17.4 %. The six graph models cannot move at all: each is fixed to METR-LA's 207 sensors.",
  "why": "Dhaka's speeds jump: 6.5-13.1 % of 5-minute steps change by more than 30 % of free-flow, against 0.7 % on a median METR-LA sensor-day and 0.0 % on PEMS-BAY. 2 of 3 sites vary more than a median Los Angeles sensor-day (coefficient of variation BM 0.51, BS 0.19, INT 0.49 against 0.229, and 0.1621 in the Bay Area); 3 of 3 remember less an hour ahead (autocorrelation at 60 minutes 0.08-0.3 against 0.3572 and 0.5766). So what a model learns from Los Angeles's smooth roads does not carry: XGBoost trained on METR-LA is 72.7 % worse than persistence in Dhaka at 60 minutes, while moving it to the Bay Area costs 17.4 %. And the models that win in Los Angeles (D2STGNN 3.779 against persistence 6.506 at 60 minutes) learned months of each sensor's history; a Dhaka camera site has one recorded day, so there is nothing to train them on, and only zero-shot models can be used."
 },
